    lesson: 'Module 7 gave the commission — build what outlasts you. This session hands the builder a practical, modern road and shows how to walk it The Way. The frameworks come from the entrepreneur Daniel Priestley, whose long-form teaching Darrell brought to this house as build input; we take what agrees with the Word, we name what is heuristic as heuristic, and where the culture’s version over-reaches, the Word corrects that claim while the useful practice underneath still stands. Begin with the ORDER, because Scripture gives one for builders: "Prepare thy work without, and make it fit for thyself in the field; and afterwards build thine house" (Proverbs 24:27) — the income-producing field comes before the house, the proof before the spend. Jesus made the same order a warning: the tower-builder who did not first count the cost becomes the neighborhood’s cautionary tale (Luke 14:28-30). Priestley’s practice here is one we adopt whole: BEFORE you build, run the smallest honest test that produces real names — a waiting list people actually join, a discussion group of the people you hope to serve, a needs analysis offered as genuine first value. If nobody signs the list, the market just saved you your savings; if a hundred do, you build on proof. Our one bend is honesty itself: the list must be real, the scarcity true — a manufactured countdown is a lie wearing marketing clothes. Next, the GOOD NAME. "A good name is rather to be chosen than great riches" (Proverbs 22:1), and Jesus put the lamp on the candlestick — visible, "that they may see your good works, and glorify your Father which is in heaven" (Matthew 5:15-16). Priestley teaches that strangers extend trust after real exposure — his 7-11-4 rule of thumb: around seven hours of your teaching, across eleven touchpoints, on four platforms. We teach that number for what it is: an attributed practitioner heuristic, not Scripture and not research we have verified — the durable truth under it is that trust is EARNED in accumulated, genuine value, which is sowing (Galatians 6:9). And the correction the Word will not waive: you are not a "personal brand." Identity is settled in Christ; what you steward in public is a good NAME, and the praising mouth belongs to someone you served, not to you (Proverbs 27:2). Third, the CERTAIN SOUND: "if the trumpet give an uncertain sound, who shall prepare himself to the battle?" (1 Corinthians 14:8). Priestley’s five-word intro frame — Name (who you are), Same (what you are like, so people can place you), Fame (what genuinely distinguishes you), Aim (what you are building now), Game (the long vision) — is nothing more than clarity, and clarity is kindness to every listener deciding whether they can trust you. Fourth, the LEVERAGE LADDER: skilled labor trades hours for wages — honorable, and the ceiling is your own two hands. The ladder climbs: turn skill into INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY (write it, teach it), IP into MEDIA (distribute it), media into DATA and SOFTWARE (systems that serve people while you sleep). Scripture honors the craftsman’s summit — "Seest thou a man diligent in his business? he shall stand before kings" (Proverbs 22:29) — and the talents parable already rebuked burying what was meant to multiply. This family has walked the ladder in front of you: the skills became teaching, the teaching became media and courses, and the courses became PoeTech itself — software serving the church and the family. Fifth, SOW LONG, IN WISE COMPANY. Priestley’s "double down on what works" and "change your environment" are older than he is: "In the morning sow thy seed, and in the evening withhold not thine hand: for thou knowest not whether shall prosper" (Ecclesiastes 11:6); "who hath despised the day of small things?" (Zechariah 4:10); "He that walketh with wise men shall be wise" (Proverbs 13:20). Put yourself in the room where building is normal — and remember module 4 already named the first room: the Body, pooling its strength. Over the whole road stands the guard of James 4: the merchant’s plan — "we will go into such a city, and buy and sell, and get gain" — is corrected not for ambition but for autonomy: "ye ought to say, If the Lord will, we shall live, and do this, or that" (James 4:13-15). The soul still comes first (module 1). The enterprise exists to serve — "There is that scattereth, and yet increaseth; and there is that withholdeth more than is meet, but it tendeth to poverty" (Proverbs 11:24) — so giving real value first is not a funnel trick; it is the Word’s own economy, and the reason this whole playbook can be run with a clean conscience. These same plays are wired into the app’s Client Growth engine with the guardrails built in, so what you learned tonight you can run tomorrow, honestly, on your own calling.',
